#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193681} :activity/id #uuid "b28c80a9-d473-57b6-bbf1-5ee7b422145c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049217 :topic/id #uuid "e446a53c-cc70-58f0-b872-8d6e7a9c434e" :topic/math-academy-id 4123} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193505 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192684 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192389 :question/id #uuid "2393d607-0f73-59f3-9a5f-ebe45050dda9" :question/math-academy-id "e-10106"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d363c839-34b6-55c7-85d8-f2013e08cc62" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048615}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441781}] :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Normal Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "f15a8c24-10c0-50c8-9701-f1e1122791bf" :step/math-academy-id 38121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193681 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193897 :tutorial/content "Likelihood functions of samples drawn from populations modeled using continuous random variables are defined similarly to discrete variables. The only difference is that we use probability density functions instead of probability mass functions.\n\nIf $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed *continuous* random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta),$ then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots , X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\nFor example, let $X_1, X_2, X_3$ be continuous, I.I.D. random variables with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = \\theta x^{-\\theta-1}, \\qquad x \\geq 1\n$$\n\nHere, $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter. Suppose we conduct our random sample and get the following results:\n\n$$\nx_1 = 2.5, \\qquad x_2 = 3.2, \\qquad x_3 = 1.2.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn this case, we compute the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\theta {x}_{i}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & =(\\theta {x}_{1}^{-\\theta -1})\\cdot (\\theta {x}_{2}^{-\\theta -1})⋯(\\theta {x}_{n}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}({x}_{1}^{-\\theta -1})\\cdot ({x}_{2}^{-\\theta -1})⋯({x}_{n}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}{({x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}⋯{x}_{n})}^{-\\theta -1} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})^{-\\theta -1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have $n=3$, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =2.5\\cdot 3.2\\cdot 1.2=9.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\theta^{3} ( 9.6 )^{-\\theta-1}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5e339526-e303-5047-8def-188a04dca982" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7001 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7a3131cc-aeb5-5d42-af4a-921157386704" :step/math-academy-id 42268 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193687}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193685 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192797 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192521 :question/id #uuid "2ff20920-30c5-5027-b461-31b13d2031e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-11121"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6068f85b-0ea6-5161-a8d1-d4eb2504852b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050018}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441787}] :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Exponential Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "9623ba3e-7523-5921-af5c-689ea499d4b4" :step/math-academy-id 42283 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193692}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193687 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192799 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192523 :question/id #uuid "bd63ff93-762d-5d7d-be8d-9701130b9fe5" :question/math-academy-id "e-11123"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbba4fae-4e47-5a69-a6c1-d8754c18767a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049638}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441788}] :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Continuous Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "bc09f530-58a5-566a-a408-bd7b51bd761e" :step/math-academy-id 42301 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193685}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193692 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193902 :tutorial/content "The probability density function of a normally distributed random variable $X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-\\frac12\\left(\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\right)^2}.\n$$\n\nSome example normal distribution curves are shown below. \n\n![](images/c9/c9359fc84401aabb19e526755b70647cb0496fa8ddba65974466e0ffc3dbbbdc.png)\n\nIf we set $\\sigma=1,$ then $f(x)$ simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-(x-\\mu)^2/2}.\n$$\n\nThis is a standard normal random variable whose mean is shifted right by $\\mu$ units.\n\nFinally, setting the unknown parameter as $\\theta$ when discussing likelihood functions is common. Following this convention, we set $\\mu=\\theta,$ and we get\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta) =  \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-(x-\\theta)^2/2}.\n$$\n\nLet's use this to compute some likelihood functions of some normally distributed sample data." :tutorial/id #uuid "8e08170d-c55e-531f-a8b9-99dfa8ecbac7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7019 :tutorial/title "The Normal Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "5912947a-0cd6-5e5a-958c-a4fff1dab140" :step/math-academy-id 42321 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193505}}] :activity/title "Likelihood Functions for Continuous Probability Distributions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192932}]}
